    tags: ['System integration', 'Mechanical design', 'Team leadership'],
    cover: {key: 'baker-bot', alt: 'The waist-up Baker Bot on a workbench, with its head, arms and control electronics assembled.', caption: 'Baker Bot during assembly!', position: '50% 31%'},
    summary: 'Leading a 12-person student build of an AI-enabled co-lecturer for Professor Baker, while independently developing the robot’s expressive head.',
    motion: {key: 'baker-head-test', title: 'Jaw and Eyelids Bench Test', navLabel: 'Head motion test', description: 'A close up bench test of the head mechanisim I developed during the project. The eyelids blink, and jaw opens/closes. All fit within the form factor of a human head!', caption: 'Head subsystem bench test. This video shows the jaw and eyelid movements. AI control is still in development'},
    context: 'The project was initiated by Professor Baker, a physics professor at Western University, to create a co-lecturer robot that could assist with teaching. The robot needed to be expressive, responsive and capable of interacting with students in a classroom setting. The end design was a humaonoid robot that you can speak to, hear responses through classroom AUX systems, and see body language that is tied to the conversation. It uses an AI model to listen and generate speech, and motorized eyelids, jaw, elbows, and 3 DOF neck to show body language that is tied to the connotation of the conversation. I was the project lead for this initiative, and worked hard to bring the vision of Professor Baker to life. During the summer, I coordinated teams of students located across Ontario to ensure all parts of the robot came together seamlessly at the end of the summer. Additionally, I took on the challenge of designing and building the head, which required clever layout of mechanical and electrical components to fit within the proportions of a human head while allowing for expressive movements.',
    contributions: ['Project lead: Coordinate the subteams, team leads and overall integration.', 'Designed the heads mechanical and electrical systems', 'Coordinated mechanical, electrical and software interfaces across a distributed team.', 'Write a small amount of C++ to move the head components for bench top testing.'],
    sections: [
      {title: 'Designing the Head', paragraphs: ['Designing the head was a unique challenge. While not an overly complex mechanism, it was difficult to fit all the needed actuators within the form factor of a human head to actuate the eyelids and jaw. The jaw was especially difficult to design since it’s pivot point is located in line with the mouth cavity. Any direct driven motors would have to be located inside the mouth cavity which would look unnatural. More thought was required', 'The solution was to use a small servo with herringbone gears to drive the jaw from an offset 180 degree servo. I used a gear ratio of 2:1 to increase the effective torque of the servo so that I could use a smaller servo. I measured my own jaws range of motion and decided the robots jaw only needed to move approximately 50 degrees, so losing a little bit of range for extra torque wasn’t a big deal.', 'One failure mode I recognized was excessive wear on the jaw gears over time could cause the mechanism to fail. To combat wear on these gears I had two strategies to prolong their service life. First, I 3D printed the gears out of ONYX, a PA6 (nylon) 15% CF infill filament that has self lubricating and abrasion resistance properties. Second, I decided to use herrignbone gears over classic spur gears. The herringbone gears have a double helix tooth profile that allows for more teeth to be in contact at any given time, which distributes the load and reduces wear. The herringbone gears also have the added benefit of canceling out axial thrust, which is a common issue with helical gears. This design choice was crucial in ensuring the longevity and reliability of the jaw mechanism.', 'The eyelids were actuated using a small SG90 servo each. This gave me the ability to control each eyelid separately which allows for more unique expressions (such as winking). The eyelids utilized a simple linkage fasioned out of a paperclip to transfer rotational movement from the servo that is set deep in the head to the eyelid itself.', 'The head was designed in OnShape, and the mechanical components were 3D printed in PLA+, PETG, and ONYX. My favourite part of designing the head was making the designing the 3D printed parts to be designed for the manufacturing process and easily assemblable. I designed the parts to use minimal supports, to have bottom chamfers to get rid of the elephants foot, to have teardrop holes to minimize bridge drooping, and so much more! I also designed the head to be modular and easily assemblable, so that if a part broke or needed to be replaced, it could be done without having to reprint the entire head. All wires have a wire groove they sit in, and they also have groves that make assembly and disasembly easier.', 'Overall, designing the head was a challenging but rewarding experience. It required careful consideration of mechanical design, electronics, and aesthetics to create a functional and expressive component of the Baker Bot.']},
      {title: 'Designing separately, assembling together', paragraphs: ['This project was primarily completed during the summer, and our team lives across Ontario with a final assembly and integration to be completed at the end of the summer before the robot was handed off to software for testing. Detailed CAD and electronics planning helped us work through integrations before final assembly. As the project lead, I created the team structure which consisted of teams (mechanical, electrical, and software) and subteams (embedded, power systems, head, neck, arms, and body). Each sub team was responsible for a different section of the robot, and my role was to coordinate the integration of all sub systems on the robot. Each sub-team designed, tested, and assembled their own sub system sperately across Ontario. I facilitated each part to come together, and for efficient use of resources between teams. This means I knew every nut and bolt on the robot, every chamfer and hole size to make sure there would be no surprises during final assembly. At the end of the summer this structure, detailed CAD, planning, and methodology paid off, the robot came together with minimal issues.']},
      {title: 'The remaining integration', paragraphs: ['The robot has motorized elbows, neck rotation and multidirectional tilt, a jaw and eyelids. Its hardware combines a Jetson, ESP32 control and custom PCBs. Mechanical and electrical work is complete, and the subsystems have been tested separately.', 'The remaining work connects embedded motor control, gesture scheduling and emotional/context cues from the cloud AI. My personal programming contribution so far is limited to head-movement methods; the wider software pipeline is a team effort and is not yet a finished demonstration.']}
    ],
    outcome: {title: 'Hardware Complete. Software in Progress.', text: 'The built subsystems are working individually. Coordinated movement and AI driven context-to-gesture control remain in progress. The goal is to have all subsystems working together for a demonstration in December 2026.'},
    exploded: {key: 'baker-head-exploded', alt: 'Exploded CAD view of the Baker Bot head showing the shell, jaw, eyelids, servos, gears, linkages, internal frame and fasteners.', caption: 'Exploded CAD of the Baker Bot head showing how all of the mechanical and electrical components fit together inside the human-head form factor.', fit: 'contain'},
